ism\": \"The aldehyde forms an imine with ammonia, cyanide adds to the iminium carbon, and proton transfer gives the alpha-aminonitrile.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"A heteroaryl acetaldehyde should remain competent in Strecker chemistry, and the alpha-aminonitrile is a robust prebiotic storage form of the amino acid skeleton. This step also naturally produces a racemic center, as expected prebiotically.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_012\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrolysis of histidine nitrile to histidine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_012\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_013\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"360-430K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"high\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"warm aqueous hydrolysis during geothermal or hydrothermal cycling\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Stepwise nitrile hydration and hydrolysis pass through an amide stage to the carboxylic acid.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Late hydrolysis of the amino nitrile is prebiotically attractive because nitriles concentrate and persist more readily than free amino acids. Moderate hydrothermal processing can then release histidine as the final product.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"name\": \"Glyceraldehyde surface oxidation plus aqueous Debus route\",\n      \"description\": \"The glyceraldehyde branch of the glycerose pool is converted to methylglyoxal by surface photothermal chemistry, then taken through aqueous ammonium imidazole synthesis and the common homologation-Strecker-hydrolysis sequence.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_011\", \"rxn_012\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_003\", \"mol_005\", \"mol_009\", \"mol_010\", \"mol_011\", \"mol_012\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P2\",\n      \"name\": \"Glyceraldehyde 