centrated shoreline residue"}, "mechanism": "Glyceraldehyde enolizes and undergoes dehydration and photoredox oxidation to the alpha-dicarbonyl methylglyoxal.", "reasoning": "Glyceraldehyde is a notably labile triose and readily degrades to methylglyoxal during warm drying and irradiation. This supplies a direct surface entry to the substituted 1,2-dicarbonyl hub needed for imidazole formation."}, {"id": "rxn_003", "name": "Hydrothermal dehydrogenative rearrangement of glyceraldehyde to methylglyoxal", "inputs": ["mol_003"], "outputs": ["mol_005"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["magnetite"], "conditions": {"temperature": "380-430K", "pressure": "high", "catalyst": "magnetite", "aqueous": "true", "other": "subseafloor hydrothermal fluid; moderate pH; mixed-valence iron surfaces"}, "mechanism": "Mixed-valence iron mineral surfaces promote triose enolization and dehydrogenative rearrangement to the alpha-dicarbonyl.", "reasoning": "Triose sugars are highly labile under hydrothermal heating and can rearrange to alpha-dicarbonyls; magnetite offers a plausible redox-active surface for that conversion. This gives a chemically justified subseafloor alternative to dry-state surface degradation."}, {"id": "rxn_004", "name": "Surface dehydration of dihydroxyacetone to methylglyoxal", "inputs": ["mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_005"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["silica"], "conditions": {"temperature": "330-370K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": "silica", "aqueous": "false", "other": "hot-spring or evaporitic dry-down; low water activity"}, "mechanism": "Keto-enol tautomerization followed by dehydration and rearrangement yields methylglyoxal.", "reasoning": "Dihydroxyacetone is particularly prone to enolization and conversion to methylglyoxal during dry heating. This creates a second surface source of the key dicarbonyl from the same glycerose pool."}, {"id": "rxn_005", "name": "Hydrothermal conversion of dihydroxyacetone to methylglyoxal", "inputs": ["mol_004"], "outputs": ["mol_005"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["magnetite"], "conditions": {"temperature": "380-420K", "pressure": "high", "catalyst": "magnetite", "aqueous": "true", "other": "subseafloor hydrothermal heating; mixed-valence iron surfaces"}, "mechanism": "Dihydroxyacetone undergoes enolization and mineral-assisted dehydration/rearrangement to methylglyoxal.", "reasoning": "Dihydroxyacetone also rearranges to methylglyoxal under moderate hydrothermal heating. This gives an environmentally distinct alternative without changing the carbon inventory."}, {"id": "rxn_006", "name": "Aqueous Debus-type synthesis of 4(5)-methylimidazole", "inputs": ["mol_005", "mol_001", "mol_006"], "outputs": ["mol_009"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": [], "conditions": {"temperature": "320-360K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": "true", "other": "ammonium-rich concentrated pool with intermittent evaporation"}, "mechanism": "Debus-Radziszewski multicomponent condensation of a substituted 1,2-dicarbonyl, a C1 aldehyde, and ammonia gives the aromatic imidazole after cyclization and dehydration.", "reasoning": "Methylglyoxal naturally places the methyl substituent at the 4/5 position of the imidazole, matching the regiochemical requirement for histidine more closely than routes that start from unsubstituted imidazole. Because 4- and 5-substitution are tautomerically related in 1H-imidazole, 4(5)-methylimidazole is an appropriate precursor."}, {"id": "rxn_007", "name": "Formamide-film synthesis of 4(5)-methylimidazole", "inputs": ["mol_005", "mol_001", "mol_008"], "outputs": ["mol_009"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": [], "conditions": {"temperature": "340-390K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": null, "aqueous": "false", "other": "formamide-rich volcanic hot-spring film; low water activity"}, "mechanism": "Heating in formamide promotes carbonyl activation, imine formation, cyclodehydration, and aromatization to the imidazole.", "reasoning": "For