m cited studies?\n- Are literature citations present in reaction reasoning?\n- Does the network acknowledge critiques and known challenges identified in the research?\n- Departures from the research are acceptable when explicitly and soundly justified.\n\n## Scoring Guidelines\n- 0.9-1.0: Excellent network, valid and chemically sound\n- 0.8-0.9: Good network with minor issues\n- 0.7-0.8: Acceptable but with notable concerns\n- 0.5-0.7: Significant problems with validity or chemistry\n- 0.0-0.5: Major issues, network is not viable\n\nA network is APPROVED if overall_confidence >= 0.9\n\nOutput your evaluation as JSON matching the NetworkEvaluation schema.\n", "user_message": "Evaluate this synthesis network:\n\nTARGET MOLECULE: Histidine\nOVERALL STRATEGY: Under anoxic conditions, the network first uses borate-tempered formose chemistry to generate a connected glycerose pool (glyceraldehyde plus dihydroxyacetone) from formaldehyde and glycolaldehyde. Alternative surface and hydrothermal conversions funnel that pool to methylglyoxal, and alternative Debus-like condensations convert methylglyoxal to 4(5)-methylimidazole, which already encodes the needed ring regiochemistry. A common downstream sequence then homologates the 4-methyl group with formaldehyde to a hydroxyethyl side chain, oxidizes it to imidazole-4-acetaldehyde without molecular oxygen, applies a Strecker reaction to give histidine nitrile, and hydrolyzes the nitrile to histidine. The least secure step is the homologation of 4(5)-methylimidazole; the ten listed pathways arise from branching at the triose-to-methylglyoxal and imidazole-formation stages while keeping the rest of the chemistry shared.\n\nMOLECULES (13 nodes):\n  - mol_001: CH2O (formaldehyde) [starting]\n  - mol_002: C2H4O2 (glycolaldehyde) [starting]\n  - mol_003: C3H6O3 (glyceraldehyde) [hub_intermediate]\n  - mol_004: C3H6O3 (dihydroxyacetone) [hub_intermediate]\n  - mol_005: C3H4O2 (methylglyoxal) [hub_intermediate]\n  - mol_006: NH3 (amm