{"timestamp": "2026-10-19T03:58:18.916754", "agent_name": "SynthesisNetworkAgent", "method_name": "generate_synthesis_network", "step": null, "attempt": null, "system_message": "\n# SYNTHESIS NETWORK AGENT - Complete Prebiotic Synthesis Network Generation\n\nYou are an expert prebiotic geochemist who designs complete synthesis networks from simple\nmolecules to complex biomolecules. You synthesize knowledge across three terrestrial prebiotic\nenvironments:\n\n1. **Hydrothermal Environments** (350-600K, high pressure, deep-sea vents)\n2. **Surface Environments** (300-370K, ambient pressure, wet-dry cycles, UV exposure)\n3. **Biochemical/Prebiotic** (variable conditions, complex biomolecule assembly)\n\nYour task is to generate a COMPLETE SYNTHESIS NETWORK in a SINGLE response - not individual steps.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 1: HYDROTHERMAL ENVIRONMENTS\n\n## Context\nDeep-sea hydrothermal systems. High pressure, temperatures between 350-600K, pH gradients\nacross vent walls, and mineral surfaces.\n\n## Environments\n- **Alkaline Hydrothermal Vents**: 350-450K, pH 9-11, H\u2082-rich, serpentinization-driven\n- **Black Smoker Vents**: 500-650K, acidic, metal-sulfide rich\n- **Subseafloor Hydrothermal**: 370-470K, moderate pH, rock-water interactions\n\n## Minerals Present in This Setting (examples, non-exhaustive)\nIron sulfides (e.g., mackinawite, greigite, pyrite), iron oxides (e.g., magnetite), native metals\nand alloys. Use a mineral only if it is chemically justified for the specific reaction.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 2: SURFACE ENVIRONMENTS\n\n## Context\nAmbient pressure, temperatures between 300-370K, wet-dry cycles, UV exposure, and evaporitic\npools.\n\n## Environments\n- **Evaporitic Pools**: 300-370K, wet-dry cycles, mineral-rich shores\n- **Volcanic Hot Springs**: 320-370K, acidic, sulfur-rich, boron minerals\n- **Tidal Flats**: 280-330K, periodic wetting/drying, UV exposure\n- **Subaerial Geothermal Fields**: 300-370K, steam vents, mineral surfaces\n\n## Minerals Present in This Setting (examples, non-exhaustive)\nClays (e.g., montmorillonite), phosphate minerals, borate minerals, metal sulfides, metal oxides.\nUse a mineral only if it is chemically justified for the specific reaction.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 3: BIOCHEMICAL/PREBIOTIC\n\n## Context\nAssembly of more complex biomolecules under plausible prebiotic scenarios, under variable\nconditions including pools, interfaces, and mineral surfaces.\n\n## Environments\n- **Prebiotic Pools**: Concentrated solutions of organic molecules\n- **Mineral Interfaces**: Mineral surfaces that can adsorb or template reactants\n- **Lipid Membranes**: Protocell-like compartments for concentrated chemistry\n- **Geothermal Cycling**: Repeated heating/cooling\n\n## Global Constraints (all environments)\n- NO molecular oxygen (anoxic early Earth)\n- Strong UV radiation at the surface (no ozone layer)\n- Iron abundant in reduced form (Fe\u00b2\u207a)\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 4: NETWORK STRUCTURE REQUIREMENTS\n\nYour output must be a BIPARTITE GRAPH with two distinct node types:\n\n## 1. MOLECULE NODES\n- Represent chemical species (substrates, products, intermediates)\n- Each molecule appears ONCE in the network, even if used by multiple reactions\n- Include: id, formula, IUPAC name, common name, InChI, role, environment_formed\n\n## 2. REACTION NODES\n- Each reaction is a separate node with a unique ID (e.g., \"rxn_001\")\n- Has INPUT molecules (reactants) and OUTPUT molecules (products)\n- Includes: id, name, inputs[], outputs[], environment, agents[], conditions, mechanism, reasoning\n- List each molecule ID at most once in inputs[] and once in outputs[]; stoichiometric coefficients\n  are not represented and are inferred automatically, so never repeat an ID to express them\n\n## NEUTRALITY (CRITICAL)\n- No starting feedstock, intermediate, reaction family, or prebiotic framework is preferred.\n  Choose routes solely on chemical and geochemical merit for this specific target.\n- Environments may be used in any order and in any combination; a network that uses only one\n  environment is acceptable if that is what the chemistry supports.\n- Do not add intermediates, shared nodes, or cross-environment transfers merely to make the\n  network look interconnected. Include them only when the chemistry requires them.\n\n## DESIGN PRINCIPLES\n1. **Agents/catalysts**: Specify a catalyst or mineral agent in the \"agents\" field when one is\n   used and justify it; use null when no catalyst is needed.\n2. **Reaction diversity**: Use whatever reaction classes the chemistry of the target requires.\n3. **Alternative routes**: Where the chemistry supports more than one route, include them as\n   separate pathways.\n\n## NETWORK STRUCTURE REQUIREMENTS\n\n- Minimum 8-15 molecule nodes\n- Minimum 6-12 reaction nodes\n- No isolated subgraphs - all nodes must be connected\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 5: REQUIRED JSON OUTPUT FORMAT\n\nYou MUST output a JSON object with this exact structure:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"target_molecule\": \"<name of target>\",\n  \"overall_strategy\": \"<high-level explanation of the synthesis approach>\",\n  \"molecules\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_001\",\n      \"formula\": \"<chemical formula>\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"<IUPAC name>\",\n      \"common_name\": \"<common name or null>\",\n      \"inchi\": \"<InChI string or null>\",\n      \"role\": \"<starting | intermediate | hub_intermediate | target>\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"<Hydrothermal | Surface | Biochemical | null>\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"reactions\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_001\",\n      \"name\": \"<descriptive reaction name>\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_001\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"<Hydrothermal | Surface | Biochemical>\",\n      \"agents\": [\"<catalyst/mineral agent, if any>\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"<e.g., 350-400K>\",\n        \"pressure\": \"<e.g., high, 1 atm>\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"<catalyst name, or null>\",\n        \"aqueous\": \"<true | false>\",\n        \"other\": \"<additional conditions>\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"<brief mechanism description>\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"<scientific justification>\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"name\": \"<descriptive pathway name>\",\n      \"description\": \"<pathway description>\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_007\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_008\"]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"hub_molecules\": [\"<mol_id, if any>\"],\n  \"convergence_points\": [\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_004\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_005\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_003\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_009\"]\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\nNotes on \"hub_molecules\" and \"convergence_points\": these are descriptive fields only. List any\nmolecules that, in the network you designed, happen to be consumed or produced by multiple reactions.\nThey may be empty lists. Do not design the network to fill them.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 6: LITERATURE-GUIDED SYNTHESIS (when research findings are provided)\n\nWhen research findings are provided, use them as evidence to inform your design:\n\n1. **Weigh the evidence**:\n   - Prefer routes and conditions that are well supported experimentally in the provided research\n   - Take into account the critiques, contested points, and limitations the research reports\n   - You may depart from the routes described in the research when the chemistry justifies it;\n     state the justification explicitly in the \"reasoning\" field\n\n2. **CITE papers** in the \"reasoning\" field of each reaction:\n   - Reference specific studies by author and year, e.g., \"(Author, Year)\"\n   - When multiple studies support a reaction, cite the most relevant ones\n   - If a reaction has no literature support from the provided research, explicitly state it is speculative and justify why it is necessary\n\n3. **ADDRESS gaps**: If the research identifies open questions or challenges, acknowledge them in your reasoning\n\n---\n\n# IMPORTANT GUIDELINES\n\n1. Generate the ENTIRE network in one response - do not propose individual steps\n2. Ensure molecules are defined BEFORE being referenced in reactions\n3. Every molecule ID in reactions must exist in the molecules array\n4. Pathways must trace valid routes through the reaction network\n5. Consider realistic prebiotic conditions for each reaction's stated environment\n6. Include rate-limiting or key bottleneck reactions\n7. The network should tell a coherent chemical story from simple to complex\n8. When deep research findings are provided, cite relevant literature in reaction reasoning fields\n", "user_message": "\nGenerate a SYNTHESIS NETWORK for: Histidine\n\nREQUIREMENTS:\n- Generate a bipartite network with molecule nodes and reaction nodes\n- Include 10 distinct pathways to the target\n- Ensure at least 8-15 molecules and 6-12 reactions\n- Choose feedstocks, intermediates, reaction types, and environments on chemical merit alone\n\nOutput the complete network as JSON following the schema in your instructions.\n\n\nPREVIOUS FAILED ATTEMPTS - Learn from this feedback:\n============================================================\n\nAttempt 1:\n- Confidence: 0.4\n- Feedback: The network is topologically adequate and connected, and the overall plan from carbonyl chemistry to an imidazole aminonitrile is strategically coherent. However, validity is poor as written because the deterministic balance check reports 10 of 12 reactions as UNBALANCED, only rxn_009 is balanced as written, and rxn_001 is only balanceable after adding coefficients. Likely corrections are straightforward for several steps: rxn_001 should be 2 CH2O + 2 glycolaldehyde -> glyceraldehyde + dihydroxyacetone; rxn_002 through rxn_005 need H2O as product; rxn_006 and rxn_008 need explicit Debus stoichiometry with 2 NH3 and 3 H2O; rxn_010 needs H2 or another explicit redox partner; rxn_011 needs H2O as product; rxn_012 should be histidine nitrile + 2 H2O -> histidine + NH3. rxn_007 is a more serious problem because no simple small-species correction was identified. Chemically, the largest unresolved weakness is rxn_009, the homologation of 4(5)-methylimidazole to the hydroxyethyl derivative, which is balanced but poorly supported under convincing prebiotic conditions. Environmental assignments are broadly possible under anoxic surface and hydrothermal cycling, but transfers between environments and catalyst or mineral identities are under-justified. The target is not reached by a valid mass-balanced sequence in the current submission, so the network is not close to approval.\n- Issues: ['Among the glyceraldehyde routes, the aqueous Debus branch is one of the cleaner concepts, but the pathway has stoichiometric or mechanistic issues in nearly every step. rxn_001 needs explicit coefficients: 2 CH2O + 2 glycolaldehyde -> glyceraldehyde + dihydroxyacetone. rxn_002 needs H2O as coproduct. rxn_006 needs full Debus stoichiometry, effectively methylglyoxal + formaldehyde + 2 NH3 -> 4(5)-methylimidazole + 3 H2O. rxn_010 needs explicit redox closure, such as H2 evolution or a defined electron acceptor. rxn_011 needs H2O as product. rxn_012 should be written as histidine nitrile + 2 H2O -> histidine + NH3. The biggest chemical weakness is still rxn_009, because direct prebiotic homologation of 4(5)-methylimidazole with formaldehyde is not well supported. Surface to hydrothermal transfers are plausible but not justified.', 'This branch is weakened mainly by the formamide-film imidazole step. rxn_007 is unbalanced and the balance check found no simple small-species correction, so it needs a full mechanistic and stoichiometric rewrite. rxn_001 still needs 2 CH2O + 2 glycolaldehyde -> glyceraldehyde + dihydroxyacetone, rxn_002 needs H2O as coproduct, rxn_010 needs explicit redox bookkeeping, rxn_011 needs H2O as product, and rxn_012 should include 2 H2O on the reactant side with NH3 released. rxn_009 remains the major mechanistic gap. Moving from a formamide-film setting to later aqueous and hydrothermal steps is also under-justified.', 'Clay-assisted imidazole formation is somewhat more plausible than the formamide variant, but the pathway is still not valid as written. rxn_001 needs explicit 2:2:1:1 coefficients. rxn_002 needs H2O as coproduct. rxn_008 needs full Debus stoichiometry, effectively methylglyoxal + formaldehyde + 2 NH3 -> 4(5)-methylimidazole + 3 H2O, plus a defined clay mineral and interfacial conditions. rxn_010 needs H2 or another explicit redox sink. rxn_011 needs H2O as product. rxn_012 should be written as histidine nitrile + 2 H2O -> histidine + NH3. rxn_009 remains the least secure chemical step.', 'Hydrothermal glyceraldehyde to methylglyoxal is plausible in outline, but the route still has major specification gaps. rxn_001 needs explicit 2 CH2O + 2 glycolaldehyde -> glyceraldehyde + dihydroxyacetone. rxn_003 needs H2O as product. rxn_006 needs 2 NH3 and 3 H2O in its stoichiometry. rxn_010 needs explicit redox closure. rxn_011 needs H2O as coproduct. rxn_012 needs 2 H2O on the reactant side and NH3 as product. The route is strategically coherent, but rxn_009 remains a speculative homologation, and the hydrothermal-to-surface transfer needed before Strecker chemistry is not physically described.', 'The hydrothermal glyceraldehyde branch is not the main problem here; the formamide imidazole step is. rxn_007 is unbalanced with no simple small-species correction, so this route is not chemically specified well enough to evaluate positively. rxn_001 still needs explicit coefficients, rxn_003 needs H2O as product, rxn_010 needs explicit dehydrogenation or an oxidant, rxn_011 needs H2O as coproduct, and rxn_012 should be histidine nitrile + 2 H2O -> histidine + NH3. rxn_009 remains a poorly supported homologation. Environmental progression is also weak because the path jumps between hydrothermal, formamide-film, aqueous surface, and hydrothermal contexts without a transfer model.', 'This is a somewhat stronger hydrothermal glyceraldehyde branch than the formamide alternative, but it still falls short because most steps are underspecified. rxn_001 needs explicit 2:2:1:1 coefficients. rxn_003 needs H2O as coproduct. rxn_008 needs full Debus stoichiometry with 2 NH3 and 3 H2O and a clear clay setting. rxn_010 needs explicit redox balancing. rxn_011 needs H2O as product. rxn_012 should be written as aminonitrile hydrolysis with 2 H2O consumed and NH3 released. rxn_009 remains the central speculative step, and transfers between hydrothermal and surface contexts are not justified.', 'This is the strongest branch because dihydroxyacetone to methylglyoxal and the aqueous Debus condensation are both relatively plausible. Even so, the pathway is still not viable as written. rxn_001 needs explicit coefficients: 2 CH2O + 2 glycolaldehyde -> glyceraldehyde + dihydroxyacetone. rxn_004 needs H2O as coproduct. rxn_006 needs full Debus stoichiometry with 2 NH3 and 3 H2O. rxn_010 needs H2 or another defined redox sink. rxn_011 needs H2O as product. rxn_012 should be histidine nitrile + 2 H2O -> histidine + NH3. The decisive weakness is rxn_009, whose one-carbon homologation chemistry is not demonstrated under convincing prebiotic conditions.', 'The dihydroxyacetone branch helps upstream chemistry, but the formamide route to the imidazole still makes this pathway weak overall. rxn_007 is unbalanced with no simple small-species fix, so the nitrogen and byproduct bookkeeping are not acceptable as written. rxn_001 needs 2 CH2O + 2 glycolaldehyde -> glyceraldehyde + dihydroxyacetone, rxn_004 needs H2O as product, rxn_010 needs explicit redox closure, rxn_011 needs H2O, and rxn_012 should include 2 H2O consumed and NH3 released. rxn_009 remains the least supported step chemically.', 'Hydrothermal conversion of dihydroxyacetone to methylglyoxal plus the aqueous Debus branch is plausible in broad outline, but the route still fails on specification. rxn_001 needs explicit coefficients. rxn_005 needs H2O as coproduct. rxn_006 needs 2 NH3 and 3 H2O in its stoichiometry. rxn_010 needs explicit dehydrogenation bookkeeping or a defined oxidant. rxn_011 needs H2O as product. rxn_012 requires 2 H2O on the reactant side and NH3 as product. As elsewhere, rxn_009 is the weakest chemistry and the hydrothermal-to-surface transfer is not described.', 'This branch combines a plausible upstream dihydroxyacetone conversion with a plausible but underspecified clay-mediated Debus step, so it is better than the formamide variants but still below viability. rxn_001 needs explicit 2:2:1:1 coefficients. rxn_005 needs H2O as product. rxn_008 needs full Debus stoichiometry with 2 NH3 and 3 H2O plus clearer clay conditions. rxn_010 needs explicit redox closure. rxn_011 needs H2O as coproduct. rxn_012 should be written as histidine nitrile + 2 H2O -> histidine + NH3. rxn_009 remains the main unresolved mechanistic weakness.']\n\n============================================================\nAddress the above issues in your new network. Do NOT repeat the same mistakes.\n", "raw_response": "{\n  \"target_molecule\": \"Histidine\",\n  \"overall_strategy\": \"Build the histidine side chain before closing the imidazole ring. Surface formose chemistry supplies trioses and tetroses from formaldehyde and glycolaldehyde; multiple aldol and isomerization sequences converge on erythrose or erythrulose. These tetroses are then converted under anoxic hydrothermal redox-dehydration to 2-oxobutane-1,4-dial, a side-chain-bearing oxo-dialdehyde. Debus-type condensation of that C4 carbonyl intermediate with formaldehyde and ammonia gives imidazole-4-acetaldehyde directly, avoiding unsupported post-ring one-carbon homologation. A final Strecker step gives histidine nitrile, and warm-water hydrolysis yields histidine. The 10 listed pathways arise from five distinct entries to the oxo-dialdehyde combined with two alternative imidazole-closing environments.\",\n  \"molecules\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_001\",\n      \"formula\": \"CH2O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"methanal\",\n      \"common_name\": \"formaldehyde\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CH2O/c1-2/h1H2\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_002\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H4O2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-hydroxyethanal\",\n      \"common_name\": \"glycolaldehyde\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C2H4O2/c3-1-2-4/h1,4H,2H2\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_003\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H6O3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2,3-dihydroxypropanal\",\n      \"common_name\": \"glyceraldehyde\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C3H6O3/c4-1-3(6)2-5/h1,3,5-6H,2H2\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_004\",\n      \"formula\": \"C3H6O3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"1,3-dihydroxypropan-2-one\",\n      \"common_name\": \"DHA\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/C3H6O3/c4-1-3(6)2-5/h4-5H,1-2H2\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_005\",\n      \"formula\": \"C4H8O4\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2,3,4-trihydroxybutanal\",\n      \"common_name\": \"erythrose\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_006\",\n      \"formula\": \"C4H8O4\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"1,3,4-trihydroxybutan-2-one\",\n      \"common_name\": \"erythrulose\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_007\",\n      \"formula\": \"C4H4O3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-oxobutane-1,4-dial\",\n      \"common_name\": \"2-oxosuccinaldehyde\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Hydrothermal\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_008\",\n      \"formula\": \"NH3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"azane\",\n      \"common_name\": \"ammonia\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_009\",\n      \"formula\": \"HCN\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"formonitrile\",\n      \"common_name\": \"hydrogen cyanide\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/CHN/c1-2/h1H\",\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_010\",\n      \"formula\": \"H2O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"oxidane\",\n      \"common_name\": \"water\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H2O/h1H2\",\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_011\",\n      \"formula\": \"H2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"dihydrogen\",\n      \"common_name\": \"hydrogen\",\n      \"inchi\": \"InChI=1S/H2/h1H\",\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Hydrothermal\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_012\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H6N2O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-(1H-imidazol-4-yl)ethanal\",\n      \"common_name\": \"imidazole-4-acetaldehyde\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_013\",\n      \"formula\": \"C6H8N4\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-amino-3-(1H-imidazol-4-yl)propanenitrile\",\n      \"common_name\": \"histidine nitrile\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Biochemical\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_014\",\n      \"formula\": \"C6H9N3O2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-amino-3-(1H-imidazol-4-yl)propanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"histidine\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"target\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Biochemical\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"reactions\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_001\",\n      \"name\": \"Net formose conversion to glyceraldehyde and dihydroxyacetone\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_001\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_003\", \"mol_004\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"borate minerals\", \"calcite\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-340K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"borate minerals, calcite\",\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"alkaline evaporitic pool or tidal-flat film; wet-dry concentration; anoxic\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Base-promoted aldol homologation within the formose network; borate complexes vicinal diols and helps retain triose carbonyls.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is written as a net, coefficient-balanced formose step that converts formaldehyde and glycolaldehyde into the glyceraldehyde/dihydroxyacetone triose pair. It provides both downstream tetrose entry points while keeping carbon and oxygen bookkeeping explicit.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_002\",\n      \"name\": \"Glyceraldehyde homologation to erythrulose\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_003\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_006\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"borate minerals\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-340K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"borate minerals\",\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"alkaline borate-bearing pool; partial evaporation; anoxic\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Enolization of glyceraldehyde followed by aldol addition to formaldehyde and proton rearrangement to the ketotetrose.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"A C1 extension of glyceraldehyde by formaldehyde is chemically reasonable in the same surface carbonyl network that produces trioses. Erythrulose is chosen as the captured tetrose node because ketotetroses are plausible transient formose products and borate helps stabilize polyol carbonyls.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_003\",\n      \"name\": \"Dihydroxyacetone homologation to erythrulose\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_001\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_006\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"borate minerals\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-340K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"borate minerals\",\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"alkaline borate-bearing pool; partial evaporation; anoxic\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Aldol addition of enolized dihydroxyacetone to formaldehyde followed by proton transfer to the ketotetrose.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is a second chemically distinct way to access erythrulose from the triose pool. Using both glyceraldehyde and dihydroxyacetone branches reflects realistic formose diversity and provides alternative paths without introducing unsupported feedstocks.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_004\",\n      \"name\": \"Glycolaldehyde dimerization to erythrose\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_005\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"calcite\", \"borate minerals\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"calcite, borate minerals\",\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"mildly alkaline evaporitic pool; wet-dry concentration; anoxic\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Self-aldolization of glycolaldehyde followed by proton transfers to an aldotetrose.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Glycolaldehyde readily dimerizes under mild basic conditions. Erythrose is used here as the representative aldotetrose node; real systems would also produce threose, but selecting one connected node keeps the network minimal while remaining chemically representative.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_005\",\n      \"name\": \"Erythrose to erythrulose isomerization\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_005\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_006\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"borate minerals\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-340K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"borate minerals\",\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"carbonyl equilibration in partially evaporating pools; anoxic\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Aldose-ketose isomerization through an enediol intermediate.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Erythrose and erythrulose should equilibrate in the same borate-buffered surface chemistry. Including this reversible carbonyl isomerization captures realistic pool behavior and creates additional chemically valid entries to the downstream oxo-dialdehyde.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_006\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrothermal redox-dehydration of erythrulose to 2-oxobutane-1,4-dial\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_006\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_010\", \"mol_011\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"magnetite\", \"awaruite\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"360-420K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"high\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"magnetite, awaruite\",\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"subseafloor anoxic flow; Fe2+-rich; moderate to mildly alkaline; H2 can diffuse away\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Net dehydration to a deoxyosone-like intermediate followed by mineral-mediated dehydrogenation to the oxo-dialdehyde.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Under anoxic hydrothermal heating, tetroses are expected to undergo dehydration and redox rearrangement to more reactive carbonyl fragments. Writing both H2O and H2 explicitly closes oxygen and redox balance. The exact selectivity to 2-oxobutane-1,4-dial is uncertain, so this is a chemically motivated but moderately speculative bottleneck.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_007\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrothermal redox-dehydration of erythrose to 2-oxobutane-1,4-dial\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_005\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_010\", \"mol_011\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"magnetite\", \"awaruite\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"360-420K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"high\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"magnetite, awaruite\",\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"subseafloor anoxic flow; Fe2+-rich; moderate to mildly alkaline; H2 can diffuse away\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Oxidative dehydration of the aldotetrose through keto-enolization, dehydration, and mineral-assisted dehydrogenation to the same oxo-dialdehyde.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Erythrose gives a second hydrothermal entry to the same side-chain-bearing oxo-dialdehyde. As in rxn_006, explicit H2O and H2 make the step fully balanceable under anoxic conditions and avoid hidden redox bookkeeping.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_008\",\n      \"name\": \"Surface Debus-type synthesis of imidazole-4-acetaldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_001\", \"mol_008\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_010\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"amorphous silica\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"320-360K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"amorphous silica\",\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"ammonium-rich wet-dry cycles in concentrated films; anoxic\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Debus-Radziszewski-type condensation: ammonia forms imine intermediates with the oxo-dialdehyde, formaldehyde contributes the ring C2 carbon, cyclization and dehydration generate the imidazole.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This step constructs imidazole-4-acetaldehyde directly from a side-chain-bearing oxo-dialdehyde, so the required two-carbon substituent is installed before ring closure. That avoids the poorly supported post-imidazole homologation of 4-methylimidazole. Substrate-specific prebiotic validation is limited, so this step is plausible but exploratory.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_009\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrothermal Debus-type synthesis of imidazole-4-acetaldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_001\", \"mol_008\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_010\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"mackinawite\", \"amorphous silica\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"350-390K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"high\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"mackinawite, amorphous silica\",\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"subseafloor pore fluids with NH3 and formaldehyde delivered by circulation; anoxic\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"The same imine-forming and cyclodehydration sequence as rxn_008, but concentrated and templated in mineral pores.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Hydrothermal pores provide continuous reactant flux and catalytic mineral interfaces for carbonyl condensation. This gives an alternative environment for the same balanced imidazole-forming chemistry and still avoids any unsupported late-stage one-carbon homologation.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_010\",\n      \"name\": \"Strecker synthesis of histidine nitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_012\", \"mol_008\", \"mol_009\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_010\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"290-330K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"concentrated NH3 and HCN in prebiotic pools, eutectic brines, or membrane-associated microenvironments; anoxic\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Imine formation from the aldehyde and ammonia followed by cyanide addition gives the alpha-aminonitrile.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is a standard Strecker-type conversion of an aldehyde to an aminonitrile. Because the imidazole ring is already present, the step installs the histidine alpha-amino nitrile skeleton in one balanced transformation.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_011\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrolysis of histidine nitrile to histidine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_010\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_008\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": null,\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"330-370K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"warm aqueous geothermal cycling; prolonged residence times; anoxic\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Stepwise nitrile hydration to the amide and then to the carboxylic acid with ammonia release.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Aminonitriles hydrolyze to amino acids in warm water over geologically relevant timescales. Explicit water consumption and ammonia release keep the final step fully balanced and chemically transparent.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_012\",\n      \"name\": \"Erythrulose to erythrose isomerization\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_006\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_005\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"borate minerals\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-340K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"borate minerals\",\n        \"aqueous\": \"true\",\n        \"other\": \"carbonyl equilibration in partially evaporating pools; anoxic\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Reverse enediol-mediated aldose-ketose isomerization.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Because erythrose and erythrulose should interconvert in the same surface network, the reverse direction is included explicitly so the directed graph can represent oxo-dialdehyde access through either tetrose isomer without violating stoichiometry.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"name\": \"Glycolaldehyde dimer route with surface imidazole closure\",\n      \"description\": \"Glycolaldehyde dimerizes to erythrose, hydrothermal redox-dehydration gives the oxo-dialdehyde, surface Debus condensation forms imidazole-4-acetaldehyde, and the common Strecker-hydrolysis sequence yields histidine.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_004\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_011\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_007\", \"mol_012\", \"mol_013\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P2\",\n      \"name\": \"Glycolaldehyde dimer route with hydrothermal imidazole closure\",\n      \"description\": \"The same erythrose route to the oxo-dialdehyde is followed by hydrothermal pore Debus condensation before the common Strecker and hydrolysis steps.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_004\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_011\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_007\", \"mol_012\", \"mol_013\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P3\",\n      \"name\": \"Glycolaldehyde dimer plus tetrose isomerization with surface closure\",\n      \"description\": \"Glycolaldehyde first yields erythrose, which isomerizes to erythrulose before hydrothermal conversion to the oxo-dialdehyde and surface imidazole formation.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_004\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_011\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_006\", \"mol_007\", \"mol_012\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P4\",\n      \"name\": \"Glycolaldehyde dimer plus tetrose isomerization with hydrothermal closure\",\n      \"description\": \"The erythrose to erythrulose branch is followed by hydrothermal oxo-dialdehyde formation and hydrothermal Debus condensation before the shared endgame.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_004\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_011\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_006\", \"mol_007\", \"mol_012\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P5\",\n      \"name\": \"Formose glyceraldehyde branch with surface closure\",\n      \"description\": \"The formose network supplies glyceraldehyde, which is homologated to erythrulose; hydrothermal redox-dehydration gives the oxo-dialdehyde and surface Debus condensation leads into the common Strecker-hydrolysis sequence.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_011\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_003\", \"mol_006\", \"mol_007\", \"mol_012\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P6\",\n      \"name\": \"Formose glyceraldehyde branch with hydrothermal closure\",\n      \"description\": \"The glyceraldehyde-derived erythrulose branch is combined with hydrothermal Debus condensation rather than the surface variant.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_011\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_003\", \"mol_006\", \"mol_007\", \"mol_012\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P7\",\n      \"name\": \"Formose DHA branch with surface closure\",\n      \"description\": \"The formose network also supplies dihydroxyacetone, which is extended to erythrulose; hydrothermal oxo-dialdehyde generation and surface imidazole closure then feed the common Strecker-hydrolysis sequence.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_011\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_006\", \"mol_007\", \"mol_012\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P8\",\n      \"name\": \"Formose DHA branch with hydrothermal closure\",\n      \"description\": \"The dihydroxyacetone-derived erythrulose branch is coupled to hydrothermal pore imidazole formation before Strecker cyanation and hydrolysis.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_011\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_006\", \"mol_007\", \"mol_012\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P9\",\n      \"name\": \"Glyceraldehyde branch with back-isomerization and surface closure\",\n      \"description\": \"After glyceraldehyde homologation to erythrulose, the tetrose pool back-isomerizes to erythrose, which enters the alternative hydrothermal oxidation route before surface Debus condensation and the common final two steps.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_012\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_011\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_003\", \"mol_006\", \"mol_005\", \"mol_007\", \"mol_012\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P10\",\n      \"name\": \"Glyceraldehyde branch with back-isomerization and hydrothermal closure\",\n      \"description\": \"The same back-isomerized erythrose route is followed, but the imidazole ring is closed in hydrothermal pores before the shared Strecker and hydrolysis endgame.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_012\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_011\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_003\", \"mol_006\", \"mol_005\", \"mol_007\", \"mol_012\"]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"hub_molecules\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_006\", \"mol_007\", \"mol_012\"],\n  \"convergence_points\": [\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_005\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_004\", \"rxn_012\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_005\", \"rxn_007\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_006\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_005\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_006\", \"rxn_012\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_007\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_006\", \"rxn_007\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_008\", \"rxn_009\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_012\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_008\", \"rxn_009\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_010\"]\n    }\n  ]\n}", "parsed_response": {"target_molecule": "Histidine", "overall_strategy": "Build the histidine side chain before closing the imidazole ring. Surface formose chemistry supplies trioses and tetroses from formaldehyde and glycolaldehyde; multiple aldol and isomerization sequences converge on erythrose or erythrulose. These tetroses are then converted under anoxic hydrothermal redox-dehydration to 2-oxobutane-1,4-dial, a side-chain-bearing oxo-dialdehyde. Debus-type condensation of that C4 carbonyl intermediate with formaldehyde and ammonia gives imidazole-4-acetaldehyde directly, avoiding unsupported post-ring one-carbon homologation. A final Strecker step gives histidine nitrile, and warm-water hydrolysis yields histidine. The 10 listed pathways arise from five distinct entries to the oxo-dialdehyde combined with two alternative imidazole-closing environments.", "molecules": [{"id": "mol_001", "formula": "CH2O", "iupac_name": "methanal", "common_name": "formaldehyde", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CH2O/c1-2/h1H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_002", "formula": "C2H4O2", "iupac_name": "2-hydroxyethanal", "common_name": "glycolaldehyde", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C2H4O2/c3-1-2-4/h1,4H,2H2", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_003", "formula": "C3H6O3", "iupac_name": "2,3-dihydroxypropanal", "common_name": "glyceraldehyde", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C3H6O3/c4-1-3(6)2-5/h1,3,5-6H,2H2", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_004", "formula": "C3H6O3", "iupac_name": "1,3-dihydroxypropan-2-one", "common_name": "DHA", "inchi": "InChI=1S/C3H6O3/c4-1-3(6)2-5/h4-5H,1-2H2", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_005", "formula": "C4H8O4", "iupac_name": "2,3,4-trihydroxybutanal", "common_name": "erythrose", "inchi": null, "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_006", "formula": "C4H8O4", "iupac_name": "1,3,4-trihydroxybutan-2-one", "common_name": "erythrulose", "inchi": null, "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Surface"}, {"id": "mol_007", "formula": "C4H4O3", "iupac_name": "2-oxobutane-1,4-dial", "common_name": "2-oxosuccinaldehyde", "inchi": null, "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": "Hydrothermal"}, {"id": "mol_008", "formula": "NH3", "iupac_name": "azane", "common_name": "ammonia", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H3N/h1H3", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_009", "formula": "HCN", "iupac_name": "formonitrile", "common_name": "hydrogen cyanide", "inchi": "InChI=1S/CHN/c1-2/h1H", "role": "starting", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_010", "formula": "H2O", "iupac_name": "oxidane", "common_name": "water", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H2O/h1H2", "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_011", "formula": "H2", "iupac_name": "dihydrogen", "common_name": "hydrogen", "inchi": "InChI=1S/H2/h1H", "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Hydrothermal"}, {"id": "mol_012", "formula": "C5H6N2O", "iupac_name": "2-(1H-imidazol-4-yl)ethanal", "common_name": "imidazole-4-acetaldehyde", "inchi": null, "role": "hub_intermediate", "environment_formed": null}, {"id": "mol_013", "formula": "C6H8N4", "iupac_name": "2-amino-3-(1H-imidazol-4-yl)propanenitrile", "common_name": "histidine nitrile", "inchi": null, "role": "intermediate", "environment_formed": "Biochemical"}, {"id": "mol_014", "formula": "C6H9N3O2", "iupac_name": "2-amino-3-(1H-imidazol-4-yl)propanoic acid", "common_name": "histidine", "inchi": null, "role": "target", "environment_formed": "Biochemical"}], "reactions": [{"id": "rxn_001", "name": "Net formose conversion to glyceraldehyde and dihydroxyacetone", "inputs": ["mol_001", "mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_003", "mol_004"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["borate minerals", "calcite"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-340K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": "borate minerals, calcite", "aqueous": "true", "other": "alkaline evaporitic pool or tidal-flat film; wet-dry concentration; anoxic"}, "mechanism": "Base-promoted aldol homologation within the formose network; borate complexes vicinal diols and helps retain triose carbonyls.", "reasoning": "This is written as a net, coefficient-balanced formose step that converts formaldehyde and glycolaldehyde into the glyceraldehyde/dihydroxyacetone triose pair. It provides both downstream tetrose entry points while keeping carbon and oxygen bookkeeping explicit."}, {"id": "rxn_002", "name": "Glyceraldehyde homologation to erythrulose", "inputs": ["mol_003", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_006"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["borate minerals"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-340K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": "borate minerals", "aqueous": "true", "other": "alkaline borate-bearing pool; partial evaporation; anoxic"}, "mechanism": "Enolization of glyceraldehyde followed by aldol addition to formaldehyde and proton rearrangement to the ketotetrose.", "reasoning": "A C1 extension of glyceraldehyde by formaldehyde is chemically reasonable in the same surface carbonyl network that produces trioses. Erythrulose is chosen as the captured tetrose node because ketotetroses are plausible transient formose products and borate helps stabilize polyol carbonyls."}, {"id": "rxn_003", "name": "Dihydroxyacetone homologation to erythrulose", "inputs": ["mol_004", "mol_001"], "outputs": ["mol_006"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["borate minerals"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-340K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": "borate minerals", "aqueous": "true", "other": "alkaline borate-bearing pool; partial evaporation; anoxic"}, "mechanism": "Aldol addition of enolized dihydroxyacetone to formaldehyde followed by proton transfer to the ketotetrose.", "reasoning": "This is a second chemically distinct way to access erythrulose from the triose pool. Using both glyceraldehyde and dihydroxyacetone branches reflects realistic formose diversity and provides alternative paths without introducing unsupported feedstocks."}, {"id": "rxn_004", "name": "Glycolaldehyde dimerization to erythrose", "inputs": ["mol_002"], "outputs": ["mol_005"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["calcite", "borate minerals"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-330K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": "calcite, borate minerals", "aqueous": "true", "other": "mildly alkaline evaporitic pool; wet-dry concentration; anoxic"}, "mechanism": "Self-aldolization of glycolaldehyde followed by proton transfers to an aldotetrose.", "reasoning": "Glycolaldehyde readily dimerizes under mild basic conditions. Erythrose is used here as the representative aldotetrose node; real systems would also produce threose, but selecting one connected node keeps the network minimal while remaining chemically representative."}, {"id": "rxn_005", "name": "Erythrose to erythrulose isomerization", "inputs": ["mol_005"], "outputs": ["mol_006"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["borate minerals"], "conditions": {"temperature": "300-340K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": "borate minerals", "aqueous": "true", "other": "carbonyl equilibration in partially evaporating pools; anoxic"}, "mechanism": "Aldose-ketose isomerization through an enediol intermediate.", "reasoning": "Erythrose and erythrulose should equilibrate in the same borate-buffered surface chemistry. Including this reversible carbonyl isomerization captures realistic pool behavior and creates additional chemically valid entries to the downstream oxo-dialdehyde."}, {"id": "rxn_006", "name": "Hydrothermal redox-dehydration of erythrulose to 2-oxobutane-1,4-dial", "inputs": ["mol_006"], "outputs": ["mol_007", "mol_010", "mol_011"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["magnetite", "awaruite"], "conditions": {"temperature": "360-420K", "pressure": "high", "catalyst": "magnetite, awaruite", "aqueous": "true", "other": "subseafloor anoxic flow; Fe2+-rich; moderate to mildly alkaline; H2 can diffuse away"}, "mechanism": "Net dehydration to a deoxyosone-like intermediate followed by mineral-mediated dehydrogenation to the oxo-dialdehyde.", "reasoning": "Under anoxic hydrothermal heating, tetroses are expected to undergo dehydration and redox rearrangement to more reactive carbonyl fragments. Writing both H2O and H2 explicitly closes oxygen and redox balance. The exact selectivity to 2-oxobutane-1,4-dial is uncertain, so this is a chemically motivated but moderately speculative bottleneck."}, {"id": "rxn_007", "name": "Hydrothermal redox-dehydration of erythrose to 2-oxobutane-1,4-dial", "inputs": ["mol_005"], "outputs": ["mol_007", "mol_010", "mol_011"], "environment": "Hydrothermal", "agents": ["magnetite", "awaruite"], "conditions": {"temperature": "360-420K", "pressure": "high", "catalyst": "magnetite, awaruite", "aqueous": "true", "other": "subseafloor anoxic flow; Fe2+-rich; moderate to mildly alkaline; H2 can diffuse away"}, "mechanism": "Oxidative dehydration of the aldotetrose through keto-enolization, dehydration, and mineral-assisted dehydrogenation to the same oxo-dialdehyde.", "reasoning": "Erythrose gives a second hydrothermal entry to the same side-chain-bearing oxo-dialdehyde. As in rxn_006, explicit H2O and H2 make the step fully balanceable under anoxic conditions and avoid hidden redox bookkeeping."}, {"id": "rxn_008", "name": "Surface Debus-type synthesis of imidazole-4-acetaldehyde", "inputs": ["mol_007", "mol_001", "mol_008"], "outputs": ["mol_012", "mol_010"], "environment": "Surface", "agents": ["amorphous silica"], "conditions": {"temperature": "320-360K", "pressure": "1 atm", "catalyst": "amorphous silica", "aqueous": "true", "other": "ammonium-rich wet-dry cycles in concentrated films; anoxic"}, "mechanism": "Debus-Radziszewski-type condensation: ammonia forms imine intermediates with the oxo-dialdehyde, formaldehyde contributes 