{"timestamp": "2026-10-19T04:07:08.339394", "agent_name": "CriticAgent", "method_name": "evaluate_network", "step": null, "attempt": null, "system_message": "You are evaluating a COMPLETE SYNTHESIS NETWORK, not individual reactions.\n\n## Neutrality\n- Do not reward or penalize a network for using (or not using) any particular feedstock, intermediate,\n  reaction family, or prebiotic framework. Judge only the evidence and the chemistry.\n- The number of shared/hub molecules, the number of convergence points, and the order or number of\n  environments used are NOT scoring criteria.\n\n## Evaluation Criteria\n\n### 1. Network Validity (0.0-1.0)\n- Are there sufficient molecule and reaction nodes?\n- Is the network connected (no isolated subgraphs)?\n- Are molecule IDs used consistently, and are reactions approximately balanced?\n- When a DETERMINISTIC ATOM/CHARGE BALANCE CHECK section is provided, treat it as ground truth for\n  mass and charge conservation; reactions it reports as UNBALANCED are conservation violations.\n\n### 2. Chemical Feasibility (0.0-1.0)\n- Is each reaction chemically sound?\n- Are the conditions appropriate for each environment?\n- Are the mechanisms plausible?\n\n### 3. Environmental Consistency (0.0-1.0)\n- Are each reaction's conditions (temperature, pressure, pH, catalysts, energy source) consistent with its stated environment?\n- If molecules are transferred between environments, is the transfer physically justified?\n- If a catalyst or mineral agent is cited, is it realistic and present in that environment?\n\n### 4. Pathway Coherence (0.0-1.0)\n- Do pathways form valid routes to the target?\n- Are key intermediates properly identified?\n- Is the overall strategy sound?\n\n### 5. Target Achievement (0.0-1.0)\n- Does the network actually produce the target molecule?\n- Are the final steps chemically valid?\n\n### 6. Literature Alignment (0.0-1.0) - When research findings are provided\n- Are reactions supported by experimental evidence from cited studies?\n- Are literature citations present in reaction reasoning?\n- Does the network acknowledge critiques and known challenges identified in the research?\n- Departures from the research are acceptable when explicitly and soundly justified.\n\n## Scoring Guidelines\n- 0.9-1.0: Excellent network, valid and chemically sound\n- 0.8-0.9: Good network with minor issues\n- 0.7-0.8: Acceptable but with notable concerns\n- 0.5-0.7: Significant problems with validity or chemistry\n- 0.0-0.5: Major issues, network is not viable\n\nA network is APPROVED if overall_confidence >= 0.9\n\nOutput your evaluation as JSON matching the NetworkEvaluation schema.\n", "user_message": "Evaluate this synthesis network:\n\nTARGET MOLECULE: Histidine\nOVERALL STRATEGY: Build the histidine side chain before closing the imidazole ring. Surface formose chemistry supplies trioses and tetroses from formaldehyde and glycolaldehyde; multiple aldol and isomerization sequences converge on erythrose or erythrulose. These tetroses are then converted under anoxic hydrothermal redox-dehydration to 2-oxobutane-1,4-dial, a side-chain-bearing oxo-dialdehyde. Debus-type condensation of that C4 carbonyl intermediate with formaldehyde and ammonia gives imidazole-4-acetaldehyde directly, avoiding unsupported post-ring one-carbon homologation. A final Strecker step gives histidine nitrile, and warm-water hydrolysis yields histidine. The 10 listed pathways arise from five distinct entries to the oxo-dialdehyde combined with two alternative imidazole-closing environments.\n\nMOLECULES (14 nodes):\n  - mol_001: CH2O (formaldehyde) [starting]\n  - mol_002: C2H4O2 (glycolaldehyde) [starting]\n  - mol_003: C3H6O3 (glyceraldehyde) [intermediate]\n  - mol_004: C3H6O3 (DHA) [intermediate]\n  - mol_005: C4H8O4 (erythrose) [hub_intermediate]\n  - mol_006: C4H8O4 (erythrulose) [hub_intermediate]\n  - mol_007: C4H4O3 (2-oxosuccinaldehyde) [hub_intermediate]\n  - mol_008: NH3 (ammonia) [starting]\n  - mol_009: HCN (hydrogen cyanide) [starting]\n  - mol_010: H2O (water) [hub_intermediate]\n  - mol_011: H2 (hydrogen) [intermediate]\n  - mol_012: C5H6N2O (imidazole-4-acetaldehyde) [hub_intermediate]\n  - mol_013: C6H8N4 (histidine nitrile) [intermediate]\n  - mol_014: C6H9N3O2 (histidine) [target]\n\nREACTIONS (12 nodes):\n  - rxn_001: mol_001, mol_002 -> mol_003, mol_004 (Net formose conversion to glyceraldehyde and dihydroxyacetone) [Surface]\n  - rxn_002: mol_003, mol_001 -> mol_006 (Glyceraldehyde homologation to erythrulose) [Surface]\n  - rxn_003: mol_004, mol_001 -> mol_006 (Dihydroxyacetone homologation to erythrulose) [Surface]\n  - rxn_004: mol_002 -> mol_005 (Glycolaldehyde dimerization to erythrose) [Surface]\n  - rxn_005: mol_005 -> mol_006 (Erythrose to erythrulose isomerization) [Surface]\n  - rxn_006: mol_006 -> mol_007, mol_010, mol_011 (Hydrothermal redox-dehydration of erythrulose to 2-oxobutane-1,4-dial) [Hydrothermal]\n  - rxn_007: mol_005 -> mol_007, mol_010, mol_011 (Hydrothermal redox-dehydration of erythrose to 2-oxobutane-1,4-dial) [Hydrothermal]\n  - rxn_008: mol_007, mol_001, mol_008 -> mol_012, mol_010 (Surface Debus-type synthesis of imidazole-4-acetaldehyde) [Surface]\n  - rxn_009: mol_007, mol_001, mol_008 -> mol_012, mol_010 (Hydrothermal Debus-type synthesis of imidazole-4-acetaldehyde) [Hydrothermal]\n  - rxn_010: mol_012, mol_008, mol_009 -> mol_013, mol_010 (Strecker synthesis of histidine nitrile) [Biochemical]\n  - rxn_011: mol_013, mol_010 -> mol_014, mol_008 (Hydrolysis of histidine nitrile to histidine) [Biochemical]\n  - rxn_012: mol_006 -> mol_005 (Erythrulose to erythrose isomerization) [Surface]\n\nPATHWAYS (10):\n  - P1: Glycolaldehyde dimer route with surface imidazole closure (5 steps)\n  - P2: Glycolaldehyde dimer route with hydrothermal imidazole closure (5 steps)\n  - P3: Glycolaldehyde dimer plus tetrose isomerization with surface closure (6 steps)\n  - P4: Glycolaldehyde dimer plus tetrose isomerization with hydrothermal closure (6 steps)\n  - P5: Formose glyceraldehyde branch with surface closure (6 steps)\n  - P6: Formose glyceraldehyde branch with hydrothermal closure (6 steps)\n  - P7: Formose DHA branch with surface closure (6 steps)\n  - P8: Formose DHA branch with hydrothermal closure (6 steps)\n  - P9: Glyceraldehyde branch with back-isomerization and surface closure (7 steps)\n  - P10: Glyceraldehyde branch with back-isomerization and hydrothermal closure (7 steps)\n\n\n=== DETERMINISTIC ATOM/CHARGE BALANCE CHECK (RDKit) ===\nComputed programmatically from each molecule's InChI (via RDKit) or stated formula. Reactions list no coefficients, so a reaction counts as balanced if some positive integer coefficients (<= 24) conserve every element and net charge.\nSummary: 12 reactions | 7 balanced as written | 5 balanceable with coefficients | 0 UNBALANCED | 0 unchecked\n  - rxn_001 balanceable: 2 CH2O + 2 C2H4O2 -> C3H6O3 + C3H6O3\n  - rxn_004 balanceable: 2 C2H4O2 -> C4H8O4\n  - rxn_008 balanceable: C4H4O3 + CH2O + 2 NH3 -> C5H6N2O + 3 H2O\n  - rxn_009 balanceable: C4H4O3 + CH2O + 2 NH3 -> C5H6N2O + 3 H2O\n  - rxn_011 balanceable: C6H8N4 + 2 H2O -> C6H9N3O2 + NH3\nTreat this check as ground truth for mass and charge conservation. Reactions that cannot be balanced violate conservation: reflect this in chemical_feasibility and reaction_scores, list them in problematic_reactions, and name the specific reaction IDs (with the missing species, if hinted) in your feedback so the network can be corrected. Note that implausibly large inferred coefficients may also indicate a mis-specified reaction.\n=== END BALANCE CHECK ===\n\n\nPlease evaluate the network and respond with JSON:\n{\n    \"pathway_evaluations\": [\n        {\n            \"pathway_id\": \"<id>\",\n            \"reaction_scores\": {\"rxn_001\": 0.85, ...},\n            \"chemical_feasibility\": <0.0-1.0>,\n            \"pathway_coherence\": <0.0-1.0>,\n            \"environmental_progression\": <0.0-1.0>,\n            \"targ