istry up to mol_012 is not persuasive enough for high confidence.", "approved": false, "problematic_reactions": ["rxn_001", "rxn_007", "rxn_009", "rxn_011"], "suggestions": ["Write rxn_001 explicitly as 2 mol_001 + 2 mol_002 -> mol_003 + mol_004", "Provide a reason to prefer rxn_012 plus rxn_007 over the more direct erythrulose route", "Correct rxn_009 to mol_007 + mol_001 + 2 mol_008 -> mol_012 + 3 mol_010 and rxn_011 to mol_013 + 2 mol_010 -> mol_014 + mol_008"]}], "best_pathway_id": "P4", "overall_confidence": 0.67, "network_feedback": "Network validity is reasonably strong: the graph is connected, molecule IDs are consistent, and the RDKit check reports 0 UNBALANCED reactions. Five reactions still need explicit coefficients: rxn_001 should be 2 mol_001 + 2 mol_002 -> mol_003 + mol_004; rxn_004 should be 2 mol_002 -> mol_005; rxn_008 and rxn_009 should be mol_007 + mol_001 + 2 mol_008 -> mol_012 + 3 mol_010; rxn_011 should be mol_013 + 2 mol_010 -> mol_014 + mol_008. The overall strategy is coherent and does reach histidine, but the network is limited by the weak support for rxn_006 and rxn_007, which require a demanding oxidation-deoxygenation sequence from tetroses to mol_007 with H2 evolution and no specified catalyst or literature backing. Debus-type closure in rxn_008 and rxn_009 is reasonable in principle, but mol_007 carries an extra aldehyde that is likely to compete in imine and polymerization chemistry, so selectivity to mol_012 is uncertain. rxn_010 and rxn_011 are more plausible, yet the Biochemical environment should be replaced with explicit nonenzymatic aqueous conditions, and transfers of reactive intermediates between surface and hydrothermal settings should be justified. Best current pathway is P4, but the network remains well below approval confidence."}, "reasoning": "", "duration_ms": 407295.6986427307, "success": true, "error_message": null}
{"timestamp": "2026-10-19T04:13:55.641797", "agent_name": "SynthesisNetworkAgent", "method_name": "generate_synthesis_network", "step": null, "attempt": null, "system_message": "\n# SYNTHESIS NETWORK AGENT - Complete Prebiotic Synthesis Network Generation\n\nYou are an expert prebiotic geochemist who designs complete synthesis networks from simple\nmolecules to complex biomolecules. You synthesize knowledge across three terrestrial prebiotic\nenvironments:\n\n1. **Hydrothermal Environments** (350-600K, high pressure, deep-sea vents)\n2. **Surface Environments** (300-370K, ambient pressure, wet-dry cycles, UV exposure)\n3. **Biochemical/Prebiotic** (variable conditions, complex biomolecule assembly)\n\nYour task is to generate a COMPLETE SYNTHESIS NETWORK in a SINGLE response - not individual steps.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 1: HYDROTHERMAL ENVIRONMENTS\n\n## Context\nDeep-sea hydrothermal systems. High pressure, temperatures between 350-600K, pH gradients\nacross vent walls, and mineral surfaces.\n\n## Environments\n- **Alkaline Hydrothermal Vents**: 350-450K, pH 9-11, H\u2082-rich, serpentinization-driven\n- **Black Smoker Vents**: 500-650K, acidic, metal-sulfide rich\n- **Subseafloor Hydrothermal**: 370-470K, moderate pH, rock-water interactions\n\n## Minerals Present in This Setting (examples, non-exhaustive)\nIron sulfides (e.g., mackinawite, greigite, pyrite), iron oxides (e.g., magnetite), native metals\nand alloys. Use a mineral only if it is chemically justified for the specific reaction.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 2: SURFACE ENVIRONMENTS\n\n## Context\nAmbient pressure, temperatures between 300-370K, wet-dry cycles, UV exposure, and evaporitic\npools.\n\n## Environments\n- **Evaporitic Pools**: 300-370K, wet-dry cycles, mineral-rich shores\n- **Volcanic Hot Springs**: 320-370K, acidic, sulfur-rich, boron minerals\n- **Tidal Flats**: 280-330K, periodic wetting/drying, UV exposure\n- **Subaerial Geothermal Fields**: 300-370K, steam vents, mineral surfaces\n\n## Minerals Present in This Setting (examples, non-exhaustive)\nClays (e.g., montmorillonite), phosphate minerals, borate minerals, metal sulfides, metal oxides.\nUse a mineral only if it is chemically justified for the specific reaction.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 3: BIOCHEMICAL/PREBIOTIC\n\n## Context\nAssembly of more complex biomolecules under plausible prebiotic scenarios, under variable\nconditions including pools, interfaces, and mineral surfaces.\n\n## Environments\n- **Prebiotic Pools**: Concentrated solutions of organic molecules\n- **Mineral Interfaces**: Mineral surfaces that can adsorb or template reactants\n- **Lipid Membranes**: Protocell-like compartments for concentrated chemistry\n- **Geothermal Cycling**: Repeated heating/cooling\n\n## Global Constraints (all environments)\n- NO molecular oxygen (anoxic early Earth)\n- Strong UV radiation at the surface (no ozone layer)\n- Iron abundant in reduced form (Fe\u00b2\u207a)\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 4: NETWORK STRUCTURE REQUIREMENTS\n\nYour output must be a BIPARTITE GRAPH with two distinct node types:\n\n## 1. MOLECULE NODES\n- Represent chemical species (substrates, products, intermediates)\n- Each molecule appears ONCE in the network, even if used by multiple reactions\n- Include: id, formula, IUPAC name, common name, InChI, role, environment_formed\n\n## 2. REACTION NODES\n- Each reaction is a separate node with a unique ID (e.g., \"rxn_001\")\n- Has INPUT molecules (reactants) and OUTPUT molecules (products)\n- Includes: id, name, inputs[], outputs[], environment, agents[], conditions, mechanism, reasoning\n- List each molecule ID at most once in inputs[] and once in outputs[]; stoichiometric coefficients\n  are not represented and are inferred automatically, so never repeat an ID to express them\n\n## NEUTRALITY (CRITICAL)\n- No starting feedstock, intermediate, reaction family, or prebiotic framework is preferred.\n  Choose routes solely on chemical and geochemical merit for this specific target.\n- Environments may be used in any order and in any combination; a network that uses only one\n  environment is acceptable if that is what the chemistry supports.\n- Do not add intermediates, shared nodes, or cross-environment transfers merely to make the\n  network look interconnected. Include them only when the chemistry requires them.\n\n## DESIGN PRINCIPLES\n1. **Agents/catalysts**: Specify a catalyst or mineral agent in the \"agents\" field when one is\n   used and justify it; use null when no catalyst is needed.\n2. **Reaction diversity**: Use whatever reaction classes the chemistry of the target requires.\n3. **Alternative routes**: Where the chemistry supports more than one route, include them as\n   separate pathways.\n\n## NETWORK STRUCTURE REQUIREMENTS\n\n- Minimum 8-15 molecule nodes\n- Minimum 6-12 reaction nodes\n- No isolated subgraphs - all nodes must be connected\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 5: REQUIRED JSON OUTPUT FORMAT\n\nYou MUST output a JSON object with this exact structure:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"target_molecule\": \"<name of target>\",\n  \"overall_strategy\": \"<high-level explanation of the synthesis approach>\",\n  \"molecules\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_001\",\n      \"formula\": \"<chemical formula>\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"<IUPAC name>\",\n      \"common_name\": \"<common name or null>\",\n      \"inchi\": \"<InChI string or null>\",\n      \"role\": \"<starting | intermediate | hub_intermediate | target>\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"<Hydrothermal | Surface | Biochemical | null>\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"reactions\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_001\",\n      \"name\": \"<descriptive reaction name>\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_001\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_003\"],\n      \"environment\": \"<Hydrothermal | Surface | Biochemical>\",\n      \"agents\": [\"<catalyst/mineral agent, if any>\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"<e.g., 350-400K>\",\n        \"pressure\": \"<e.g., high, 1 atm>\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"<catalyst name, or null>\",\n        \"aqueous\": \"<true | false>\",\n        \"other\": \"<additional conditions>\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"<brief mechanism description>\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"<scientific justification>\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"name\": \"<descriptive pathway name>\",\n      \"description\": \"<pathway description>\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_007\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_008\"]\n    }\n  ],\n  \"hub_molecules\": [\"<mol_id, if any>\"],\n  \"convergence_points\": [\n    {\n      \"molecule_id\": \"mol_004\",\n      \"incoming_reactions\": [\"rxn_002\", \"rxn_005\"],\n      \"outgoing_reactions\": [\"rxn_003\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_009\"]\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\nNotes on \"hub_molecules\" and \"convergence_points\": these are descriptive fields only. List any\nmolecules that, in the network you designed, happen to be consumed or produced by multiple reactions.\nThey may be empty lists. Do not design the network to fill them.\n\n---\n\n# SECTION 6: LITERATURE-GUIDED SYNTHESIS (when research findings are provided)\n\nWhen research findings are provided, use them as evidence to inform your design:\n\n1. **Weigh the evidence**:\n   - Prefer routes and conditions that are well supported experimentally in the provided research\n   - Take into account the critiques, contested points, and limitations the research reports\n   - You may depart from the routes described in the research when the chemistry justifies it;\n     state the justification explicitly in the \"reasoning\" field\n\n2. **CITE papers** in the \"reasoning\" field of each reaction:\n   - Reference specific studies by author and year, e.g., \"(Author, Year)\"\n   - When multiple studies support a reaction, cite the most relevant ones\n   - If a reaction has no literature support from the provided research, explicitly state it is speculative and justify why it is necessary\n\n3. **ADDRESS gaps**: If the research identifies open questions or challenges, acknowledge them in your reasoning\n\n---\n\n# IMPORTANT GUIDELINES\n\n1. Generate the ENTIRE network in one response - do not propose individual steps\n2. Ensure molecules are defined BEFORE being referenced in reactions\n3. Every molecule ID in reactions must exist in the molecules array\n4. Pathways must trace valid routes through the reaction network\n5. Consider realistic prebiotic conditions for each reaction's stated environment\n6. Include rate-limiting or key bottleneck reactions\n7. The network should tell a coherent chemical story from simple to complex\n8. When deep research findings are provided, cite relevant literature in reaction reasoning fields\n", "user_message": "\nGenerate a SYNTHESIS NETWORK for: Histidine\n\nREQUIREMENTS:\n- Generate a bipartite network with molecule nodes and reaction nodes\n- Include 10 distinct pathways to the target\n- Ensure at least 8-15 molecules and 6-12 reactions\n- Choose feedstocks, intermediates, reaction types, and environments on chemical merit alone\n\nOutput the complete network as JSON following the schema in your instructions.\n\n\nPREVIOUS FAILED ATTEMPTS - Learn from this feedback:\n============================================================\n\nAttempt 1:\n- Confidence: 0.4\n- Feedback: The network is topologically adequate and connected, and the overall plan from carbonyl chemistry to an imidazole aminonitrile is strategically coherent. However, validity is poor as written because the deterministic balance check reports 10 of 12 reactions as UNBALANCED, only rxn_009 is balanced as written, and rxn_001 is only balanceable after adding coefficients. Likely corrections are straightforward for several steps: rxn_001 should be 2 CH2O + 2 glycolaldehyde -> glyceraldehyde + dihydroxyacetone; rxn_002 through rxn_005 need H2O as product; rxn_006 and rxn_008 need explicit Debus stoichiometry with 2 NH3 and 3 H2O; rxn_010 needs H2 or another explicit redox partner; rxn_011 needs H2O as product; rxn_012 should be histidine nitrile + 2 H2O -> histidine + NH3. rxn_007 is a more serious problem because no simple small-species correction was identified. Chemically, the largest unresolved weakness is rxn_009, the homologation of 4(5)-methylimidazole to the hydroxyethyl derivative, which is balanced but poorly supported under convincing prebiotic conditions. Environmental assignments are broadly possible under anoxic surface and hydrothermal cycling, but transfers between environments and catalyst or mineral identities are under-justified. The target is not reached by a valid mass-balanced sequence in the current submission, so the network is not close to approval.\n- Issues: ['Among the glyceraldehyde routes, the aqueous Debus branch is one of the cleaner concepts, but the pathway has stoichiometric or mechanistic issues in nearly every step. rxn_001 needs explicit coefficients: 2 CH2O + 2 glycolaldehyde -> glyceraldehyde + dihydroxyacetone. rxn_002 needs H2O as coproduct. rxn_006 needs full Debus stoichiometry, effectively methylglyoxal + formaldehyde + 2 NH3 -> 4(5)-methylimidazole + 3 H2O. rxn_010 needs explicit redox closure, such as H2 evolution or a defined electron acceptor. rxn_011 needs H2O as product. rxn_012 should be written as histidine nitrile + 2 H2O -> histidine + NH3. The biggest chemical weakness is still rxn_009, because direct prebiotic homologation of 4(5)-methylimidazole with formaldehyde is not well supported. Surface to hydrothermal transfers are plausible but not justified.', 'This branch is weakened mainly by the formamide-film imidazole step. rxn_007 is unbalanced and the balance check found no simple small-species correction, so it needs a full mechanistic and stoichiometric rewrite. rxn_001 still needs 2 CH2O + 2 glycolaldehyde -> glyceraldehyde + dihydroxyacetone, rxn_002 needs H2O as coproduct, rxn_010 needs explicit redox bookkeeping, rxn_011 needs H2O as product, and rxn_012 should include 2 H2O on the reactant side with NH3 released. rxn_009 remains the major mechanistic gap. Moving from a formamide-film setting to later aqueous and hydrothermal steps is also under-justified.', 'Clay-assisted imidazole formation is somewhat more plausible than the formamide variant, but the pathway is still not valid as written. rxn_001 needs explicit 2:2:1:1 coefficients. rxn_002 needs H2O as coproduct. rxn_008 needs full Debus stoichiometry, effectively methylglyoxal + formaldehyde + 2 NH3 -> 4(5)-methylimidazole + 3 H2O, plus a defined clay mineral and interfacial conditions. rxn_010 needs H2 or another explicit redox sink. rxn_011 needs H2O as product. rxn_012 should be written as histidine nitrile + 2 H2O -> histidine + NH3. rxn_009 remains the least secure chemical step.', 'Hydrothermal glyceraldehyde to methylglyoxal is plausible in outline, but the route still has major specification gaps. rxn_001 needs explicit 2 CH2O + 2 glycolaldehyde -> glyceraldehyde + dihydroxyacetone. rxn_003 needs H2O as product. rxn_006 needs 2 NH3 and 3 H2O in its stoichiometry. rxn_010 needs explicit redox closure. rxn_011 needs H2O as coproduct. rxn_012 needs 2 H2O on the reactant side and NH3 as product. The route is strategically coherent, but rxn_009 remains a speculative homologation, and the hydrothermal-to-surface transfer needed before Strecker chemistry is not physically described.', 'The hydrothermal glyceraldehyde branch is not the main problem here; the formamide imidazole step is. rxn_007 is unbalanced with no simple small-species correction, so this route is not chemically specified well enough to evaluate positively. rxn_001 still needs explicit coefficients, rxn_003 needs H2O as product, rxn_010 needs explicit dehydrogenation or an oxidant, rxn_011 needs H2O as coproduct, and rxn_012 should be histidine nitrile + 2 H2O -> histidine + NH3. rxn_009 remains a poorly supported homologation. Environmental progression is also weak because the path jumps between hydrothermal, formamide-film, aqueous surface, and hydrothermal contexts without a transfer model.', 'This is a somewhat stronger hydrothermal glyceraldehyde branch than the formamide alternative, but it still falls short because most steps are underspecified. rxn_001 needs explicit 2:2:1:1 coefficients. rxn_003 needs H2O as coproduct. rxn_008 needs full Debus stoichiometry with 2 NH3 and 3 H2O and a clear clay setting. rxn_010 needs explicit redox balancing. rxn_011 needs H2O as product. rxn_012 should be written as aminonitrile hydrolysis with 2 H2O consumed and NH3 released. rxn_009 remains the central speculative step, and transfers between hydrothermal and surface contexts are not justified.', 'This is the strongest branch because dihydroxyacetone to methylglyoxal and the aqueous Debus condensation are both relatively plausible. Even so, the pathway is still not viable as written. rxn_001 needs explicit coefficients: 2 CH2O + 2 glycolaldehyde -> glyceraldehyde + dihydroxyacetone. rxn_004 needs H2O as coproduct. rxn_006 needs full Debus stoichiometry with 2 NH3 and 3 H2O. rxn_010 needs H2 or another defined redox sink. rxn_011 needs H2O as product. rxn_012 should be histidine nitrile + 2 H2O -> histidine + NH3. The decisive weakness is rxn_009, whose one-carbon homologation chemistry is not demonstrated under convincing prebiotic conditions.', 'The dihydroxyacetone branch helps upstream chemistry, but the formamide route to the imidazole still makes this pathway weak overall. rxn_007 is unbalanced with no simple small-species fix, so the nitrogen and byproduct bookkeeping are not acceptable as written. rxn_001 needs 2 CH2O + 2 glycolaldehyde -> glyceraldehyde + dihydroxyacetone, rxn_004 needs H2O as product, rxn_010 needs explicit redox closure, rxn_011 needs H2O, and rxn_012 should include 2 H2O consumed and NH3 released. rxn_009 remains the least supported step chemically.', 'Hydrothermal conversion of dihydroxyacetone to methylglyoxal plus the aqueous Debus branch is plausible in broad outline, but the route still fails on specification. rxn_001 needs explicit coefficients. rxn_005 needs H2O as coproduct. rxn_006 needs 2 NH3 and 3 H2O in its stoichiometry. rxn_010 needs explicit dehydrogenation bookkeeping or a defined oxidant. rxn_011 needs H2O as product. rxn_012 requires 2 H2O on the reactant side and NH3 as product. As elsewhere, rxn_009 is the weakest chemistry and the hydrothermal-to-surface transfer is not described.', 'This branch combines a plausible upstream dihydroxyacetone conversion with a plausible but underspecified clay-mediated Debus step, so it is better than the formamide variants but still below viability. rxn_001 needs explicit 2:2:1:1 coefficients. rxn_005 needs H2O as product. rxn_008 needs full Debus stoichiometry with 2 NH3 and 3 H2O plus clearer clay conditions. rxn_010 needs explicit redox closure. rxn_011 needs H2O as coproduct. rxn_012 should be written as histidine nitrile + 2 H2O -> histidine + NH3. rxn_009 remains the main unresolved mechanistic weakness.']\n\nAttempt 2:\n- Confidence: 0.67\n- Feedback: Network validity is reasonably strong: the graph is connected, molecule IDs are consistent, and the RDKit check reports 0 UNBALANCED reactions. Five reactions still need explicit coefficients: rxn_001 should be 2 mol_001 + 2 mol_002 -> mol_003 + mol_004; rxn_004 should be 2 mol_002 -> mol_005; rxn_008 and rxn_009 should be mol_007 + mol_001 + 2 mol_008 -> mol_012 + 3 mol_010; rxn_011 should be mol_013 + 2 mol_010 -> mol_014 + mol_008. The overall strategy is coherent and does reach histidine, but the network is limited by the weak support for rxn_006 and rxn_007, which require a demanding oxidation-deoxygenation sequence from tetroses to mol_007 with H2 evolution and no specified catalyst or literature backing. Debus-type closure in rxn_008 and rxn_009 is reasonable in principle, but mol_007 carries an extra aldehyde that is likely to compete in imine and polymerization chemistry, so selectivity to mol_012 is uncertain. rxn_010 and rxn_011 are more plausible, yet the Biochemical environment should be replaced with explicit nonenzymatic aqueous conditions, and transfers of reactive intermediates between surface and hydrothermal settings should be justified. Best current pathway is P4, but the network remains well below approval confidence.\n- Issues: ['Shortest route, but the direct erythrose to mol_007 step in rxn_007 is the weakest chemistry in the network. It requires oxidation, deoxygenation, and H2 evolution from a tetrose under generic hydrothermal conditions with no catalyst or literature support. rxn_008 is Debus-like in outline, but the extra aldehyde in mol_007 makes selectivity to mol_012 uncertain, and rxn_004 also needs explicit 2 mol_002 -> mol_005 stoichiometry. Histidine is reached formally, but only with modest confidence.', 'Keeping imidazole closure hydrothermal avoids transporting unstable mol_007 back to a surface, which helps this route, but rxn_007 is still the main liability. rxn_009 is reasonable in broad Debus terms yet still lacks support for this multifunctional substrate, and rxn_004 also needs explicit 2 mol_002 -> mol_005 stoichiometry. The target is reached on paper, not convincingly in chemistry.', 'More coherent than P1 because erythrulose is a better oxidation-state match to mol_007 than erythrose. The bottleneck remains rxn_006, and moving highly reactive mol_007 from hydrothermal generation to surface closure is not justified. rxn_004 still needs explicit 2 mol_002 -> mol_005 stoichiometry, and downstream closure and nitrile hydrolysis are plausible only at low to moderate confidence.', 'This is the strongest listed pathway. Glycolaldehyde dimerization followed by tetrose isomerization is plausible, erythrulose is the best tetrose entry to mol_007, and keeping rxn_006 and rxn_009 both hydrothermal reduces handling of unstable mol_007. Confidence is still capped by the speculative redox-deoxygenation in rxn_006, the likely competing imine or polymerization chemistry in rxn_009, and the missing 2 mol_002 -> mol_005 stoichiometry for rxn_004.', 'The route is weakened by rxn_001, which is a lumped formose step that generates both mol_003 and mol_004 while the pathway assumes selective continuation through mol_003. rxn_002 is also an idealized one-step homologation. The core uncertainty still lies in rxn_006 and the selectivity of rxn_008.', 'Keeping the imidazole-forming step hydrothermal helps avoid moving mol_007, but the pathway still inherits the nonselective net formose chemistry of rxn_001 and the speculative tetrose-to-mol_007 conversion. rxn_009 remains chemically possible only in broad outline for this multifunctional substrate.', 'Among the formose-derived routes, the DHA branch is somewhat cleaner because rxn_003 is a more straightforward net path to erythrulose. It still depends on the nonselective rxn_001 mixture and the weakly supported rxn_006 to make mol_007. Surface Debus closure in rxn_008 is plausible in concept but not yet well justified for this substrate.', 'This is the best of the formose-based pathways because rxn_003 gives a plausible entry to erythrulose and rxn_009 keeps the unstable mol_007 intermediate in the same hydrothermal setting where it is formed. Even so, the pathway is still dominated by the weak support for rxn_006 and the uncertain selectivity of hydrothermal Debus closure.', 'Back-isomerizing erythrulose to erythrose adds steps without solving the key chemical problem. The route still begins with a nonselective formose mixture, then relies on the less plausible direct erythrose to mol_007 conversion in rxn_007 and on a surface Debus closure that requires moving a very reactive dialdehyde between environments.', 'Keeping ring closure hydrothermal is better than P9, but the back-isomerization still adds a weak detour and the route remains constrained by rxn_007. The network reaches histidine formally, yet the chemistry up to mol_012 is not persuasive enough for high confidence.']\n\n============================================================\nAddress the above issues in your new network. Do NOT repeat the same mistakes.\n", "raw_response": "{\n  \"target_molecule\": \"Histidine\",\n  \"overall_strategy\": \"Histidine is approached through its direct Strecker precursor, imidazole-4-acetaldehyde. The network first assembles the side-chain-bearing alpha-keto dialdehyde 2-oxobutane-1,4-dial from simple C1/C2 feedstocks: formaldehyde is generated hydrothermally from CO2, glycolaldehyde is reached either by surface formose chemistry or hydrothermal hydrogenation of glyoxal, acetaldehyde comes from glycolaldehyde deoxygenation or acetylene hydration, and glyoxal plus acetaldehyde undergo aldol addition followed by dehydrogenation. Debus-Radziszewski cyclization of 2-oxobutane-1,4-dial with formaldehyde and ammonia gives imidazole-4-acetaldehyde directly, avoiding weak post-ring homologation of simpler imidazoles, and Strecker formation followed by nitrile hydrolysis yields histidine. Small mobile C1/C2 feedstocks can transfer between settings more plausibly than the unstable C4 dialdehydes, and H2O, H2, and NH3 are included explicitly so the network is mass- and redox-balanced.\",\n  \"molecules\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_001\",\n      \"formula\": \"CO2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"carbon dioxide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"carbon dioxide\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_002\",\n      \"formula\": \"H2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"dihydrogen\",\n      \"common_name\": \"hydrogen\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_003\",\n      \"formula\": \"CH2O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"methanal\",\n      \"common_name\": \"formaldehyde\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Hydrothermal\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_004\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H4O2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-hydroxyethanal\",\n      \"common_name\": \"glycolaldehyde\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_005\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H2O2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"ethanedial\",\n      \"common_name\": \"glyoxal\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_006\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"ethyne\",\n      \"common_name\": \"acetylene\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_007\",\n      \"formula\": \"C2H4O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"ethanal\",\n      \"common_name\": \"acetaldehyde\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_008\",\n      \"formula\": \"C4H6O3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-hydroxybutane-1,4-dial\",\n      \"common_name\": \"2-hydroxysuccinaldehyde\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_009\",\n      \"formula\": \"C4H4O3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-oxobutane-1,4-dial\",\n      \"common_name\": \"2-oxosuccinaldehyde\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Hydrothermal\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_010\",\n      \"formula\": \"NH3\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"azane\",\n      \"common_name\": \"ammonia\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_011\",\n      \"formula\": \"H2O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"oxidane\",\n      \"common_name\": \"water\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_012\",\n      \"formula\": \"HCN\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"hydrogen cyanide\",\n      \"common_name\": \"hydrocyanic acid\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"starting\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_013\",\n      \"formula\": \"C5H6N2O\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-(1H-imidazol-4-yl)ethanal\",\n      \"common_name\": \"imidazole-4-acetaldehyde\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_014\",\n      \"formula\": \"C6H8N4\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-amino-3-(1H-imidazol-4-yl)propanenitrile\",\n      \"common_name\": \"histidine nitrile\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"hub_intermediate\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Surface\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"mol_015\",\n      \"formula\": \"C6H9N3O2\",\n      \"iupac_name\": \"2-amino-3-(1H-imidazol-4-yl)propanoic acid\",\n      \"common_name\": \"histidine\",\n      \"inchi\": null,\n      \"role\": \"target\",\n      \"environment_formed\": \"Biochemical\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"reactions\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_001\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrothermal reduction of carbon dioxide to formaldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_001\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_003\", \"mol_011\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"magnetite\", \"awaruite\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"360-420K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"high\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"magnetite/awaruite\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"alkaline vent fluids, pH 9-10, H2-rich, anoxic\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Surface-bound CO2 is partially hydrogenated on Fe-Ni mineral surfaces to a formyl-level carbonyl; water is the coproduct of the net reduction.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Serpentinization supplies H2 and Fe-Ni minerals capable of low-level CO2 reduction under anoxic hydrothermal conditions. Formaldehyde is treated here as a diffusible C1 feedstock rather than as a high-yield product.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_002\",\n      \"name\": \"Surface formose dimerization of formaldehyde to glycolaldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_003\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_004\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"brucite\", \"borate minerals\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"320-350K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"brucite/borate\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"wet-dry cycles in mildly basic evaporitic pools\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Base-assisted formaldehyde self-condensation gives the first stable C2 sugar, glycolaldehyde.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Glycolaldehyde is the earliest persistent formose product, and borate-bearing wet-dry settings help enrich it relative to uncontrolled polymerization.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_003\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrothermal hydrogenation of glyoxal to glycolaldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_004\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"awaruite\", \"mackinawite\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"360-420K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"high\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"awaruite/mackinawite\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"subseafloor flow paths with dissolved glyoxal and H2\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"One aldehyde group of glyoxal is selectively hydrogenated on reduced mineral surfaces to the alpha-hydroxy aldehyde glycolaldehyde.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Selective partial reduction of a dicarbonyl to an alpha-hydroxy aldehyde is chemically simpler than hydrothermal C1-C1 coupling and provides an alternative C2 entry without invoking unstable tetrose chemistry.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_004\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrogenative deoxygenation of glycolaldehyde to acetaldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_004\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_007\", \"mol_011\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"awaruite\", \"mackinawite\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"370-430K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"high\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"awaruite/mackinawite\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"H2-rich subseafloor circulation\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Metal-surface hydrogenolysis/deoxygenation converts the alpha-hydroxy aldehyde into the less oxidized aldehyde.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This explicitly H2-driven deoxygenation is simpler and better balanced than unsupported sugar redox cascades, but it remains moderately speculative; the independent acetylene branch is therefore retained as a bypass.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_005\",\n      \"name\": \"Mineral-assisted hydration of acetylene to acetaldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_006\", \"mol_011\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_007\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Cu(I) sulfide\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"330-390K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Cu(I) sulfide\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"acidic volcanic hot-spring films carrying dissolved acetylene\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Metal activation of the alkyne enables water addition to an enol that tautomerizes to acetaldehyde.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Acetylene is a plausible volcanic or impact-derived feedstock. Metal-sulfide-assisted hydration gives a direct non-sugar route to acetaldehyde, although support is weaker than for classical Hg-catalyzed hydration.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_006\",\n      \"name\": \"Surface cross-aldol of glyoxal and acetaldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_007\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_008\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"montmorillonite\", \"calcite\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-330K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"montmorillonite\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"evaporative concentration at mildly basic pool margins\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Acetaldehyde enolization followed by attack on one carbonyl of glyoxal gives the beta-hydroxydialdehyde 2-hydroxybutane-1,4-dial.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This C2+C2 union installs the entire future histidine side chain before ring closure. Cross-aldol selectivity is a bottleneck, but clay/mineral adsorption and evaporation can favor the desired mixed product.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_007\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrothermal cross-aldol of glyoxal and acetaldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_005\", \"mol_007\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_008\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"green rust\", \"FeS\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"350-390K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"high\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"green rust/FeS\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"alkaline vent pores with steep thermal gradients\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Mineral-assisted acetaldehyde enolization and addition to glyoxal generates 2-hydroxybutane-1,4-dial in situ.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"A hydrothermal aldol branch avoids moving the fragile beta-hydroxydialdehyde between environments; mineral confinement can favor rapid formation and immediate consumption.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_008\",\n      \"name\": \"Dehydrogenation of 2-hydroxybutane-1,4-dial to 2-oxobutane-1,4-dial\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_008\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_002\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"greigite\", \"awaruite\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"370-420K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"high\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"greigite/awaruite\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"rapid in situ turnover favored because the keto-dialdehyde product is short-lived\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Mineral-catalyzed alcohol dehydrogenation converts the secondary alcohol into the alpha-keto group, releasing H2.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"The keto-dialdehyde is the exact oxidation-state match needed for Debus closure, and explicit H2 evolution keeps redox bookkeeping closed. The step is demanding but more defensible than leaving the oxidation implicit.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_009\",\n      \"name\": \"Surface Debus-Radziszewski cyclization to imidazole-4-acetaldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_003\", \"mol_010\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_011\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"silica\", \"phosphate minerals\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"320-350K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"ammonium-rich evaporitic films with partial drying\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Sequential imine formation with ammonia, cyclization, dehydration, and aromatization yield the imidazole while retaining the CH2CHO side chain.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"This is the key strategic step: the imidazole is built from an aldehyde-bearing alpha-keto dialdehyde so imidazole-4-acetaldehyde forms directly, avoiding weak post-ring homologation of simpler imidazoles. Competing oligomerization of the dialdehydic substrate remains the main uncertainty.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_010\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrothermal Debus-Radziszewski cyclization to imidazole-4-acetaldehyde\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_009\", \"mol_003\", \"mol_010\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_011\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Hydrothermal\",\n      \"agents\": [\"greigite\", \"silica\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"350-390K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"high\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"greigite\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"ammonium-bearing vent microcompartments with rapid quench into cooler zones\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Ammonia-driven imine formation and ring closure occur on mineral interfaces, followed by dehydration to the imidazole aldehyde.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"A hydrothermal Debus variant is less selective but physically coherent because the short-lived keto-dialdehyde can be generated and trapped in the same setting instead of being transported to the surface.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_011\",\n      \"name\": \"Strecker conversion of imidazole-4-acetaldehyde to histidine nitrile\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_013\", \"mol_010\", \"mol_012\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_011\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Surface\",\n      \"agents\": [\"phosphate minerals\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"300-320K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": null,\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"cyanide-bearing pool, near-neutral to mildly acidic pH, reduced UV exposure by shallow burial or night-side accumulation\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Imine/iminium formation from the aldehyde and ammonia is followed by cyanide addition to give the alpha-aminonitrile.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Strecker chemistry is among the most robust prebiotic entries to alpha-amino acid precursors. Imidazole-4-acetaldehyde is substantially more transportable than the upstream keto-dialdehyde, so quenching into cooler cyanide-bearing pools is plausible.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"rxn_012\",\n      \"name\": \"Hydrolysis of histidine nitrile to histidine\",\n      \"inputs\": [\"mol_014\", \"mol_011\"],\n      \"outputs\": [\"mol_015\", \"mol_010\"],\n      \"environment\": \"Biochemical\",\n      \"agents\": [\"Fe2+\", \"montmorillonite\"],\n      \"conditions\": {\n        \"temperature\": \"320-360K\",\n        \"pressure\": \"1 atm\",\n        \"catalyst\": \"Fe2+/montmorillonite\",\n        \"aqueous\": true,\n        \"other\": \"geothermal heating-cooling cycles in concentrated pools or mineral-lined compartments\"\n      },\n      \"mechanism\": \"Sequential hydration of the nitrile through amide-like intermediates yields the carboxylic acid and releases ammonia.\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Histidine nitrile can act as a kinetically persistent reservoir that hydrolyzes during prolonged wet heating. Mineral and Fe2+-assisted hydrolysis in concentrated pools converts it to the amino acid under milder conditions than the upstream carbonyl chemistry.\"\n    }\n  ],\n  \"pathways\": [\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P1\",\n      \"name\": \"Formose entry with surface aldol and vent Debus\",\n      \"description\": \"CO2-derived formaldehyde enters surface formose chemistry to give glycolaldehyde; after hydrothermal deoxygenation, the C4 aldol product is carried into hydrothermal pores for oxidation and Debus closure before Strecker conversion and hydrolysis.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_011\", \"rxn_012\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_003\", \"mol_004\", \"mol_007\", \"mol_008\", \"mol_009\", \"mol_013\", \"mol_014\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P2\",\n      \"name\": \"Formose entry with vent aldol and surface Debus\",\n      \"description\": \"Formose-derived glycolaldehyde is deoxygenated hydrothermally, then the side-chain-bearing keto-dialdehyde is assembled in vents and transferred only after oxidation for cooler surface Debus and Strecker chemistry.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_011\", \"rxn_012\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_003\", \"mol_004\", \"mol_007\", \"mol_008\", \"mol_009\", \"mol_013\", \"mol_014\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P3\",\n      \"name\": \"Formose entry with fully vent-retained C4 sequence\",\n      \"description\": \"After surface generation of glycolaldehyde, acetaldehyde formation, aldol assembly, dehydrogenation, and Debus closure all occur hydrothermally before histidine nitrile is hydrolyzed in cooler pools.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_002\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_011\", \"rxn_012\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_003\", \"mol_004\", \"mol_007\", \"mol_008\", \"mol_009\", \"mol_013\", \"mol_014\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P4\",\n      \"name\": \"Glyoxal-hydrogenation entry with surface aldol and vent Debus\",\n      \"description\": \"Glyoxal is first partially reduced hydrothermally to glycolaldehyde, then deoxygenated to acetaldehyde; the C4 aldol intermediate is made at the surface but trapped as the imidazole aldehyde in hydrothermal microcompartments.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_011\", \"rxn_012\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_003\", \"mol_004\", \"mol_007\", \"mol_008\", \"mol_009\", \"mol_013\", \"mol_014\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P5\",\n      \"name\": \"Glyoxal-hydrogenation entry with vent aldol and surface Debus\",\n      \"description\": \"Hydrothermal glyoxal reduction provides glycolaldehyde, and the C4 side-chain framework is assembled and oxidized in vents before surface Debus closure, Strecker formation, and hydrolysis.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_011\", \"rxn_012\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_003\", \"mol_004\", \"mol_007\", \"mol_008\", \"mol_009\", \"mol_013\", \"mol_014\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P6\",\n      \"name\": \"Glyoxal-hydrogenation entry with fully vent-retained C4 sequence\",\n      \"description\": \"This branch keeps the most labile carbonyl intermediates hydrothermal from glyoxal reduction through Debus closure, then uses surface Strecker chemistry and biochemical hydrolysis to reach histidine.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_003\", \"rxn_004\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_011\", \"rxn_012\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_003\", \"mol_004\", \"mol_007\", \"mol_008\", \"mol_009\", \"mol_013\", \"mol_014\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P7\",\n      \"name\": \"Acetylene entry with surface aldol and vent Debus\",\n      \"description\": \"A non-sugar branch supplies acetaldehyde directly from acetylene hydration; the resulting surface aldol product is then processed hydrothermally through oxidation and Debus closure before Strecker chemistry.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_006\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_010\", \"rxn_011\", \"rxn_012\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_003\", \"mol_007\", \"mol_008\", \"mol_009\", \"mol_013\", \"mol_014\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P8\",\n      \"name\": \"Acetylene entry with vent aldol and surface Debus\",\n      \"description\": \"Acetylene hydration provides acetaldehyde, hydrothermal aldol and dehydrogenation build the side-chain-bearing keto-dialdehyde, and cooler surface Debus plus Strecker chemistry give histidine nitrile for final hydrolysis.\",\n      \"reaction_sequence\": [\"rxn_001\", \"rxn_005\", \"rxn_007\", \"rxn_008\", \"rxn_009\", \"rxn_011\", \"rxn_012\"],\n      \"key_intermediates\": [\"mol_003\", \"mol_007\", \"mol_008\", \"mol_009\", \"mol_013\", \"mol_014\"]\n    },\n    {\n      \"pathway_id\": \"P9\",\n      \"name\": \"Acetylene entry with fully vent-retained C4 sequence\",\n      \"description\": \"The acetylene branch bypasses glycolaldehyde deoxygenation, then keeps aldol assembly, dehydrogenation, and Debus closure together in hydrot